      "payload": "{\"model_calls\":1,\"phase\":\"preparation\",\"plan\":{\"A\":\"Research a shared prime-sieve classifier. Enumerate every fragment pair, preserve its exact concatenated value (including the right fragment’s leading-zero width), find the maximum candidate, and sieve primes through its integer square root. Test candidates only with those primes, stopping when p > n/p. Preserve duplicate prime occurrences and sorted output. Submit the implementation and a correctness/performance comparison against baseline; treat this as an alternative primality engine, not an assumed integration win.\",\"B\":\"Research an integer-only wheel trial-division classifier as an independent alternative. Handle n < 2 and small prime factors, then test only 30-wheel candidate divisors using d <= n/d. Avoid sqrtl and floating-point fmod, and preserve the existing candidate multiset and output behavior. Measure it independently against baseline; do not combine it with another primality engine without measuring the combination.\",\"C\":\"Research deterministic Miller–Rabin for the contract’s bounded candidates (at most 600971707). Use a proven base set for that range, handle small values and bases divisible by n correctly, and use integer modular arithmetic whose multiplication cannot overflow for this bound. Document the deterministic bound and preserve exact output multiplicity and ordering. Report correctness and measured performance; do not use known test inputs or expected outputs.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only after comparing A, B, and C as standalone candidates against the valid, correct baseline (score 0.5; mean 0.04357344564050436 s). The unavailable-diagnostic raw log does not override the completed check_passed outcome. Use the unchanged compiler, target, benchmark, and validation process; require exact-byte correctness across the authorized cases. Choose by measured results, not theoretical or additive gains. If combining preprocessing or other changes with the best engine, benchmark that integrated program separately and retain it only if it remains correct and improves the measured result. Preserve leading-zero semantics, candidate multiplicity, and sorted output; do not specialize to individual inputs or alter tests, oracle, budgets, compiler, or target.\",\"analysis\":\"The source tests P² concatenations (at most 900 under this contract), then repeatedly builds strings and calls isprime, which uses sqrtl and floating-point fmod while checking every factor. The observed baseline is correct but has room for performance work; there is no profiling evidence establishing which alternative will win, so A/B/C should test distinct classifiers independently. The certified candidate bound permits a bounded deterministic Miller–Rabin experiment, while a shared sieve and integer wheel division provide separate alternatives. Preserve general computation for the stated contract rather than encoding sample or private-case answers. The official example’s output-count contradiction is unresolved; do not use it to infer behavior beyond the reviewed contract.\"},\"request_sha256\":\"db13860b3ca15c1b4f391cab7d5c4ccd4d1ce834d4cddfe74d982e456cd5bcc4\",\"response\":{\"data\":{\"A\":\"Research a shared prime-sieve classifier. Enumerate every fragment pair, preserve its exact concatenated value (including the right fragment’s leading-zero width), find the maximum candidate, and sieve primes through its integer square root. Test candidates only with those primes, stopping when p > n/p. Preserve duplicate prime occurrences and sorted output. Submit the implementation and a correctness/performance comparison against baseline; treat this as an alternative primality engine, not an assumed integration win.\",\"B\":\"Research an integer-only wheel trial-division classifier as an independent alternative. Handle n < 2 and small prime factors, then test only 30-wheel candidate divisors using d <= n/d. Avoid sqrtl and floating-point fmod, and preserve the existing candidate multiset and output behavior. Measure it independently against baseline; do not combine it with another primality engine without measuring the combination.\",\"C\":\"Research deterministic Miller–Rabin for the contract’s bounded candidates (at most 600971707). Use a proven base set for that range, handle small values and bases divisible by n correctly, and use integer modular arithmetic whose multiplication cannot overflow for this bound. Document the deterministic bound and preserve exact output multiplicity and ordering. Report correctness and measured performance; do not use known test inputs or expected outputs.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only after comparing A, B, and C as standalone candidates against the valid, correct baseline (score 0.5; mean 0.04357344564050436 s). The unavailable-diagnostic raw log does not override the completed check_passed outcome. Use the unchanged compiler, target, benchmark, and validation process; require exact-byte correctness across the authorized cases. Choose by measured results, not theoretical or additive gains. If combining preprocessing or other changes with the best engine, benchmark that integrated program separately and retain it only if it remains correct and improves the measured result. Preserve leading-zero semantics, candidate multiplicity, and sorted output; do not specialize to individual inputs or alter tests, oracle, budgets, compiler, or target.\",\"analysis\":\"The source tests P² concatenations (at most 900 under this contract), then repeatedly builds strings and calls isprime, which uses sqrtl and floating-point fmod while checking every factor. The observed baseline is correct but has room for performance work; there is no profiling evidence establishing which alternative will win, so A/B/C should test distinct classifiers independently. The certified candidate bound permits a bounded deterministic Miller–Rabin experiment, while a shared sieve and integer wheel division provide separate alternatives. Preserve general computation for the stated contract rather than encoding sample or private-case answers. The official example’s output-count contradiction is unresolved; do not use it to infer behavior beyond the reviewed contract.\"},\"error\":null,\"infrastructure_error\":false,\"model\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"nonce\":\"206feaff615348498afb45e9edb08220\",\"protocol\":\"hpc-forest-codex-mailbox-v1\",\"reasoning_effort\":\"max\",\"request_sha256\":\"db13860b3ca15c1b4f391cab7d5c4ccd4d1ce834d4cddfe74d982e456cd5bcc4\",\"session_id\":\"c8044062692149138d826d3f388a08ef\",\"usage\":{\"cached_tokens_included_in_input\":true,\"codex_jsonl_sha256\":\"ba6f0f674299eb91ca1da79f85a6cab5e8854e0f6a89b43adb40ca3449a68210\",\"events\":[{\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"cached_input_tokens\":0,\"input_tokens\":8265,\"output_tokens\":7921,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":7358}],\"llm_calls\":1,\"model_requested\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"reasoning_effort_requested\":\"max\",\"token_accounting\":\"exact\",\"tokens\":16186,\"tool_events\":[]}},\"status\":\"complete\"}",
