    {
      "id": 17,
      "kind": "mother_usage",
      "payload": "{\"model_calls\":1,\"phase\":\"synthesis\",\"plan\":{\"A\":\"Continue from the fastest measured standalone branch: the dense byte sieve through the integer square root of the maximum candidate (5.129 ms). Explore sieve representation and candidate traversal/layout improvements, while keeping exact integer arithmetic and prime classification. Parse each fragment’s actual decimal width so leading zeros in right fragments are preserved; classify every pair, retain duplicate prime values, and sort numerically. Benchmark against this branch on the fixed suite; do not specialize to sample or hidden values.\",\"B\":\"Develop an independent exact primality-kernel alternative. Compare the existing small-factor filter plus wheel trial division with deterministic Miller–Rabin using a base set proven sufficient for the full candidate bound, or with another exact method. Handle small values explicitly, use overflow-safe modular arithmetic, and never use probabilistic primality. Preserve correct fragment scaling, classify every pair, and retain duplicates. The measured small-factor/wheel branch was 5.482 ms, so require measured improvement over the best sieve branch rather than assuming this approach is faster.\",\"C\":\"The OpenMP variants measured about 43.73 ms versus the 43.57 ms baseline, and the fixed compile command does not enable OpenMP. Do not add compiler flags or assume parallel speedup. Instead, investigate a serial batched residue filter: precompute fragment residues for selected small primes, mark composite pair slots while handling candidate-equals-divisor cases, then apply an exact primality test to survivors. Compare this alternative with A’s sieve and B’s kernel; keep all filtering exact and preserve every prime occurrence.\",\"G\":\"Use A’s measured 5.129 ms sieve branch as the current integration baseline. Integrate at most one research-tree change per trial, then measure the integrated program directly—gains are not additive and filters may duplicate sieve work. Check exact output bytes on the research and distinct confirmation cases under the fixed compiler and target. Promote an integration only if it passes all checks and improves measured performance; otherwise retain the fastest measured correct program. Preserve actual fragment widths, classify all pairs, keep duplicates, and do not infer answers from the example or case values.\",\"analysis\":\"The baseline passed correctness at 43.573 ms. The best observed standalone result is A’s sieve branch at 5.129 ms; B’s small-factor/wheel branch measured 5.482 ms, another B variant 5.696 ms, and A’s tiled variant 5.709 ms. C’s OpenMP variants measured about 43.73 ms. No integrated-build measurements are supplied, so standalone timings do not establish combined gains. The reviewed finite contract has six research and six distinct confirmation cases; recommendations should remain general over its valid inputs, preserve right-fragment leading-zero width, test every pair exactly, retain multiplicity, and sort output. The official example’s P/output-count contradiction is unresolved and should not be used as an oracle.\"},\"request_sha256\":\"3e68ebb6c40d3fed587db5119d606aa1e0d90f4fd83ab7e5c609c054014cdef0\",\"response\":{\"data\":{\"A\":\"Continue from the fastest measured standalone branch: the dense byte sieve through the integer square root of the maximum candidate (5.129 ms). Explore sieve representation and candidate traversal/layout improvements, while keeping exact integer arithmetic and prime classification. Parse each fragment’s actual decimal width so leading zeros in right fragments are preserved; classify every pair, retain duplicate prime values, and sort numerically. Benchmark against this branch on the fixed suite; do not specialize to sample or hidden values.\",\"B\":\"Develop an independent exact primality-kernel alternative. Compare the existing small-factor filter plus wheel trial division with deterministic Miller–Rabin using a base set proven sufficient for the full candidate bound, or with another exact method. Handle small values explicitly, use overflow-safe modular arithmetic, and never use probabilistic primality. Preserve correct fragment scaling, classify every pair, and retain duplicates. The measured small-factor/wheel branch was 5.482 ms, so require measured improvement over the best sieve branch rather than assuming this approach is faster.\",\"C\":\"The OpenMP variants measured about 43.73 ms versus the 43.57 ms baseline, and the fixed compile command does not enable OpenMP. Do not add compiler flags or assume parallel speedup. Instead, investigate a serial batched residue filter: precompute fragment residues for selected small primes, mark composite pair slots while handling candidate-equals-divisor cases, then apply an exact primality test to survivors. Compare this alternative with A’s sieve and B’s kernel; keep all filtering exact and preserve every prime occurrence.\",\"G\":\"Use A’s measured 5.129 ms sieve branch as the current integration baseline. Integrate at most one research-tree change per trial, then measure the integrated program directly—gains are not additive and filters may duplicate sieve work. Check exact output bytes on the research and distinct confirmation cases under the fixed compiler and target. Promote an integration only if it passes all checks and improves measured performance; otherwise retain the fastest measured correct program. Preserve actual fragment widths, classify all pairs, keep duplicates, and do not infer answers from the example or case values.\",\"analysis\":\"The baseline passed correctness at 43.573 ms. The best observed standalone result is A’s sieve branch at 5.129 ms; B’s small-factor/wheel branch measured 5.482 ms, another B variant 5.696 ms, and A’s tiled variant 5.709 ms. C’s OpenMP variants measured about 43.73 ms. No integrated-build measurements are supplied, so standalone timings do not establish combined gains. The reviewed finite contract has six research and six distinct confirmation cases; recommendations should remain general over its valid inputs, preserve right-fragment leading-zero width, test every pair exactly, retain multiplicity, and sort output. The official example’s P/output-count contradiction is unresolved and should not be used as an oracle.\"},\"error\":null,\"infrastructure_error\":false,\"model\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"nonce\":\"b7169e0d18884578a8a4237aa7ec71f0\",\"protocol\":\"hpc-forest-codex-mailbox-v1\",\"reasoning_effort\":\"max\",\"request_sha256\":\"3e68ebb6c40d3fed587db5119d606aa1e0d90f4fd83ab7e5c609c054014cdef0\",\"session_id\":\"c8044062692149138d826d3f388a08ef\",\"usage\":{\"cached_tokens_included_in_input\":true,\"codex_jsonl_sha256\":\"d242c5c6672ec17924913c8dee7f56af7fd8fca1015475d9c584a4486cb7643f\",\"events\":[{\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"cached_input_tokens\":0,\"input_tokens\":24093,\"output_tokens\":7007,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":6413}],\"llm_calls\":1,\"model_requested\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"reasoning_effort_requested\":\"max\",\"token_accounting\":\"exact\",\"tokens\":31100,\"tool_events\":[]}},\"status\":\"complete\"}",
